# Rust
/target/
**/target/
# Reviewer agents build into ad-hoc dirs like target-m3-review/ (CARGO_TARGET_DIR
# overrides) — those are multi-GB and must never be staged. `target/` alone did
# not match them, and one `git add -A` tried to commit ~9GB of artifacts.
target-*/
**/target-*/
# ./build/ was scripts/build.sh's output directory (native + cross binaries of
# the legacy Bevy companion). Both the script and that crate are archived on
# branch attic/legacy-rust-and-fleet (ADR 0020); the rule stays so a checkout of
# that branch, or any ad-hoc build dir, still cannot stage binaries.
/build/

# Release archives from scripts/build-release.sh (the self-contained Go
# binary — app/public/ and app/assets/ are embedded into it, EMBED-1 — plus
# license files) — never commit these either.
/dist/

# The two live Rust workspaces (desktop/src-tauri/ and app-rs/) commit their
# Cargo.lock deliberately — they build applications, where a locked dependency
# tree is the convention. The root workspace's Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock are
# archived on branch attic/legacy-rust-and-fleet (ADR 0020); the target rules
# above are still needed for the two live ones.

# fleetsmith — per-developer runtime tier, never committed

# local save data, if ever written into the repo during dev testing
saves/
*.save.json

# personal Claude Code overrides (autopilot permissions) — never committed
.claude/settings.local.json

# Python bytecode cache (visual-check.py gets imported by test probes)
__pycache__/

# Frontend build tooling (Frontend architecture track F1, docs/plan/ROADMAP.md)
# — app/frontend/src/*.ts is the source of truth; the compiled bundle it
# produces is committed at app/public/js/dexel.js (see app/frontend/README.md).
app/frontend/node_modules/

# The README quick start's `go build -o dexel .` output — a build artifact.
# Released binaries come from scripts/build-release.sh into /dist.
/app/dexel
/app/dexel.exe

# macOS Finder metadata
.DS_Store
